  "articles": [
    {
      "sequence_id": 1,
      "text": "7. Sumimasen  'Excuse me,' 'I'm sorry,' or 'Thank you'\n\nIn English, when you address a stranger in order to elicit some information (getting directions, for example), or when you want to get the attention of a clerk at a store, you first say 'Excuse me.' A comparable Japanese expression used for these purposes is Sumimasen. This expression can also be used when you want to apologize. For example, you can use it when you bump into a person on the street.\n\nAnother usage of this expression, which seems to be closely related to the way Japanese think, is to show gratitude. In Japanese, one way to show gratitude for a favor done for you is to say something like 'I'm sorry for the trouble you took for me,' Doomo sumimasen. We will study more about this usage in later lessons."
    }
  ],
